#
# RadiANT heart-rate node - the manufacturer reference design.
#
# One prefix for the whole application, HRM_*, the same way the dongle uses
# ANT_DONGLE_* and the simulator uses ANT_SIM_*. The three never build together,
# but a shared prefix would make "which application does this symbol belong to"
# ambiguous, and the answer to that question is how the CI assertions stay
# honest.
#
# The prefix was STRAP_* until Phase 3.2. It was renamed because "strap" named
# the bench instrument this started as, and this is now the design a
# manufacturer copies. The rename landed AFTER .github/workflows/build.yml's
# build-node job and scripts/build_all.ps1's node row, deliberately: until those
# existed nothing in the repository compiled this application at all, so a
# rename that broke the build would have stayed invisible until somebody next
# needed the board.
#

rsource "../../radiant/src/node/Kconfig"
rsource "../../radiant/src/profiles/Kconfig"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.antr_api"

# ── Defaults this application imposes on symbols it does not own ────────────
#
# BEFORE `source "Kconfig.zephyr"`, and that placement is the whole mechanism.
# Kconfig collects every `default` of a symbol in the order the definitions are
# parsed and takes the FIRST whose condition holds. nrfxlib's own definition of
# MPSL_TIMESLOT_SESSION_COUNT carries `default 0`; if this block were parsed
# after it, ours would be dead and the .config would read 0 with no error
# anywhere.
#
# WHY THE SYMBOL MATTERS HERE. Every timeslot session_open() fails when the
# count is 0, while the image still runs - so the arbitrated build's failure
# mode is a node that refuses every arm rather than a build error, and on a
# bench that is indistinguishable from a dead antenna. radiant/Kconfig's own
# help text for RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL says so at length. It used to be
# set by hand in ble.conf, which worked only for as long as somebody remembered
# to pass that fragment.
config MPSL_TIMESLOT_SESSION_COUNT
	default 1 if HRM_BLE_HRS

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

menuconfig HRM
	bool "RadiANT heart-rate node"
	default y
	help
	  A RadiANT NODE: radiant driving a master channel with
	  radiant/src/profiles/profile_hr.c on top, broadcasting ANT+ heart rate
	  (device type 0x78). No USB and no serial bridge - this is not a
	  dongle, and the only host interface is the log.

	  It exists twice over. It was P8 of the multiprotocol plan - the BLE
	  coexistence work needs a node whose ANT+ side is a master, because a
	  master owns its slot phase and a slave does not, and what happens to a
	  transmit schedule under an arbiter is the whole question. It is now
	  also the reference design a manufacturer starts from; see
	  docs/hrm-reference-design.md for what that means and what it does not.

if HRM

config HRM_DEVICE_NUMBER
	int "ANT device number when the node is not provisioned"
	range 1 65535
	default 4242
	help
	  Used only when radiant/src/node/ has no identity record. A provisioned
	  node takes its device number from that record instead, which is what
	  ADR 0009 requires - the number is a property of the node's identity,
	  not of the image. The fallback keeps a freshly-flashed DK usable on a
	  bench without a provisioning step.

	  A SHIPPED PRODUCT MUST NOT USE THE FALLBACK, and today it has no
	  choice: nothing in apps/ calls node_ident_provision(), so no board in
	  this tree can be provisioned. See docs/hrm-reference-design.md's
	  production checklist, item 3.

config HRM_MANUFACTURER_ID
	int "manufacturer id for the common pages"
	range 0 255
	default 255
	help
	  255 is the ANT+ development id. A shipped strap needs a real one from
	  the alliance; using it here would be claiming somebody else's.

# ── The sensor seam ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A CHOICE AND NOT A BOOL, AND THAT IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY. With a bool
# ("enable the simulator") a manufacturer who forgets to turn it off ships a
# chest strap that reports a constant 72 bpm from a simulator, and nothing in
# the build says so. A choice makes "I forgot to unhook the simulator"
# impossible to express: exactly one arm is compiled, exactly one
# hrm_sensor_register() call is linked, and the boot log names which.
#
# There is deliberately NO fallback from HRM_SENSOR_CUSTOM to the simulator.
# See that symbol's help.
choice HRM_SENSOR
	prompt "heart-rate source"
	default HRM_SENSOR_SIMULATED
	help
	  Which implementation of src/hrm_sensor.h's seam is compiled in. The
	  seam is push (source-driven), not poll: a real detector is an ISR on
	  an analogue front end's comparator line and knows the instant the beat
	  happened, whereas an application tick only knows when it last looked.
	  That direction is the design and not an implementation detail - see
	  src/hrm_sensor.h's header for why the Zephyr sensor API was rejected.

config HRM_SENSOR_SIMULATED
	bool "simulated - a fixed rate, no hardware"
	help
	  src/hrm_sim.c. A DK has no electrodes. The beat generator produces
	  beats at HRM_SENSOR_SIM_BPM so the page 0 event count and event time -
	  the fields that actually survive a lost packet - advance the way a
	  real strap's do.

	  This is the reference implementation of the seam as well as the bench
	  instrument: its scheduler is a pure function in src/hrm_sim_sched.c
	  precisely so that the drift property can be tested without a radio.

config HRM_SENSOR_EXAMPLE
	bool "example driver - the seam's second implementation"
	help
	  src/hrm_sensor_example.c: a worked example of a driver that owns a
	  hardware timestamp, written to be read rather than to be shipped. It
	  reports beats from a k_timer handler (interrupt context) using
	  hrm_sensor_now_1024(), which is the same shape an AFE comparator ISR
	  has.

	  IT IS BUILT IN CI ON PURPOSE. A seam with one implementation is
	  indistinguishable from a direct call with extra prototypes - the same
	  argument .github/workflows/build.yml already makes about
	  encryption.conf. The second implementation is what proves the seam is
	  a seam.

config HRM_SENSOR_CUSTOM
	bool "custom - your driver, registered at boot"
	help
	  NOTHING IN THIS TREE IMPLEMENTS THE SEAM UNDER THIS OPTION. Your code
	  calls hrm_sensor_register() before main()'s hrm_sensor_start(), from a
	  SYS_INIT() or from a driver's own init hook.

	  If it does not, the node transmits PROFILE_HR_INVALID_BPM forever and
	  logs an error at boot. That is the CORRECT failure and it is
	  deliberately NOT a fallback to the simulator: a strap that quietly
	  reports 72 bpm from a simulator on a real chest is a worse outcome
	  than one that reports nothing, because only the second is visible from
	  the receiving end.

endchoice

config HRM_SENSOR_SIM_BPM
	int "simulated heart rate, beats per minute"
	depends on HRM_SENSOR_SIMULATED || HRM_SENSOR_EXAMPLE
	range 30 220
	default 72
	help
	  Was CONFIG_STRAP_HR_BPM, and it moved under the source rather than
	  staying an application-level symbol because it is a property of ONE
	  implementation of the seam. A custom driver has a real heart to read;
	  a rate setting means nothing to it.

	  30..220 is the range profile_hr's page 0 can represent as a computed
	  value and that a receiver will believe. Note that the byte is not
	  clamped at 255 as a sentinel: on this profile 0 means "no reading" and
	  0xFF is a real 255 bpm.

config HRM_BLE_HRS
	bool "also advertise the SIG Heart Rate Service over BLE"
	depends on BT && BT_PERIPHERAL
	select BT_HRS
	#
	# SELECT, NOT DEPENDS, AND THE SYMBOL WAS CHECKED FIRST.
	#
	# `select` bypasses a symbol's own `depends on`, so selecting something
	# with non-trivial dependencies can produce an invalid configuration
	# that Kconfig only warns about. RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL has NO
	# `depends on` at all - radiant/Kconfig says so explicitly and explains
	# why (it cannot depend on RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF, because the nrf arm's
	# own `!BT` exclusion is relaxed by this symbol, and each depending on
	# the other is a Kconfig loop). So there is nothing here for `select` to
	# bypass, and `select` is the right verb.
	#
	# WHAT IT RETIRES. CONFIG_BT=y breaks the `!BT` half of the nrf
	# backend's `depends on !BT || RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL`. Without
	# the gate the choice silently falls back to RADIANT_BACKEND_NULL - a
	# node that boots, logs "transmitting", and puts nothing on the air.
	# This used to be a hand-written line in ble.conf, which meant the
	# interlock held only for as long as one fragment stayed correct.
	# CMakeLists.txt carries a FATAL_ERROR backstop for the same interlock
	# approached from the other side (CONFIG_BT without this symbol).
	select RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL
	help
	  P8b: the SoftDevice Controller beside the ANT+ master, advertising
	  the standard SIG Heart Rate Service, so that one strap serves both a
	  phone and an ANT+ receiver.

	  THE CONNECTION AND ADVERTISING INTERVALS ARE THE PRIORITY MECHANISM,
	  not a power tuning. Finding 1 of the plan: MPSL's two
	  application-visible priorities rank us against ourselves and not
	  against the Bluetooth controller, which outranks anything an
	  application can request. So RadiANT keeps its slots by SHAPING the
	  other stack's demand, exactly as nrf/samples/esb/esb_ptx_ble does
	  with a 1 s connection interval.

config HRM_BLE_ADV_INTERVAL_MS
	int "BLE advertising interval, milliseconds"
	depends on HRM_BLE_HRS
	range 100 10000
	default 1000
	help
	  1-2 s is what the plan specifies for the strap. Shortening it is not
	  a power trade, it is a decision to take air away from ANT+, and the
	  coexistence gate is what says whether it can be afforded.

	  The cost of the long interval is discovery latency, and it is
	  measured, not estimated: at 1009 ms a scanner saw 18 advertisements in
	  120 s, and both a 12 s and a 40 s scan found nothing while the node
	  was on the air. Budget >= 90 s before calling a node missing. See
	  docs/hrm-reference-design.md section 7.

config HRM_BLE_MIN_CONN_INTERVAL_UNITS
	int "shortest connection interval to accept, 1.25 ms units"
	depends on HRM_BLE_HRS
	range 6 3200
	default 800
	help
	  800 units is 1 s, which is what nrf/samples/esb/esb_ptx_ble uses to
	  run BLE beside a timeslot user, and it is chosen for the same reason.
	  A phone will ask for far less - iOS asks for tens of milliseconds -
	  and accepting hands the controller a recurring high-priority event
	  every few milliseconds for the life of the connection. MPSL gives the
	  controller priority levels above anything this application can
	  request, so that air is simply gone; refusing the update is the only
	  place the decision can be made.

config HRM_SUPPRESS_ANT_WHEN_CONNECTED
	bool "stop ANT+ while a BLE connection is live"
	depends on HRM_BLE_HRS
	default y
	help
	  P9's suppression rule, as policy above the link layer: a phone that
	  has connected is receiving the same heart rate over the SIG service,
	  so continuing to broadcast ANT+ spends air on a duplicate - and it
	  means the hard contention case never normally arises at all.

	  Turn it off for the minority-manufacturer behaviour, where straps
	  keep transmitting ANT+ while connected so a watch and a phone can be
	  served at once. That configuration is the one the coexistence gate
	  actually stresses.

module = HRM
module-str = hrm
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # HRM
